# --- s010 Book I d.1 q.3 (= Ord. I d.1 p.2 q.1, nn. 62–76)
n("n247","s010-01","vtrum fruitio sit actus voluntatis","la","source","= *Ord.* I d. 1 p. 2 q. 1, n. 62 (*an sit aliquis actus elicitus a voluntate, vel passio*). The *ad oppositum* (*Voluntas actu elicito amat Deum…*) is n. 64, verbatim.","verified")
n("n248","s010-01","10. ethicorum","la","source","Aristotle, *EN* X.4, 1174b31–33: pleasure completes the activity *like the bloom of youth*. The fruit/delight argument is answered in *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 74.","verified")
n("n249","s010-02","opinio Thome","la","source","The identity of *dilectio* and *delectatio* is the opinion reported in the note of *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 76 (*Opinio quod dilectio et delectatio sunt idem…*). The attribution to Thomas is Gorris’s; cf. *ST* I-II q. 25 a. 2 and q. 31.","to-verify")
n("n250","s010-02","in demonibus est dilectio","la","commentary","The argument from the demons, who love themselves without delight, is not in this form in the *Ordinatio* text of d. 1 p. 2 q. 1. It may come from the *Reportatio* or from Gorris himself.","uncertain")
n("n251","s010-03","Fruimur cognitis bonis in quibus voluntas propter se delectata quiescit.","la","source","Augustine, *De Trinitate* X.10.13 (*Fruimur enim cognitis, in quibus voluntas ipsis propter se ipsa delectata conquiescit*), quoted by Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 72, to show that *frui* covers both act and delight.","verified")
n("n252","s010-04","opinio Egidii in presenti distinctione","la","source","Giles of Rome, *In I Sent.* d. 1 (enjoyment as principally an act of the intellect attaining the end, secondarily delight in the will). Scotus does not name him here.","to-verify")
n("n253","s010-04","Frui est amore inherere.","la","source","Augustine, *De doctrina christiana* I.4.4: *Frui est enim amore inhaerere alicui rei propter se ipsam*. Cf. *Ord.* I d. 1 n. 162.","verified")
n("n254","s010-04","sicut subiectum et propria passio","la","commentary","Gorris’s formula for the relation of enjoyment (act) to delight (its consequent): delight is the *proper attribute* of the act, as in *EN* X pleasure follows and perfects activity.","certain")
n("n255","s010-05","vt expectatur vt gustandum","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 1 n. 74: *fructus est ultimum quod exspectatur ab arbore, non ut possidendum corporaliter, sed ut habendum per actum potentiae* (‘tasting’ is Gorris’s gloss).","verified")
n("n256","s010-h","[Questio tertia.]","la","textual","The running headline of p. 21 (where this question ends) already reads *Di.i. Q.iiii*: the headline names the question that begins on the page.","verified")
n("n257","s010-03","implies both love and delight","en","translation","*importat*, ‘implies/connotes’, in the technical sense of what a term signifies.")
n("n258","s010-04","subject and its proper attribute","en","translation","*subiectum et propria passio*: the logical pair of substrate and *proprium* (Porphyry’s fourth predicable).")
# --- B collation (full): B21R–B22R
n("n1043","s010-04","finis separatus prius non habitus","la","textual","B: *finis separatus non habitus prius* (word order).","certain")
n("n1044","s010-04","quomodo se habent ergo","la","textual","B: *quomodo ergo se habent* (word order).","certain")
upd("n255",append="B reads *vt expectatur ad possidendum* and *vt expectatur ad gustandum*.")
